-- THE ONLY FIXTURE THAT EXERCISES `cohScan`'s TWO-SLOT RETURN (F-3d, #614/#311).
-- Without it that design decision has ZERO coverage: revert `cohScan` to a single
-- classified verdict and every other gate in this tree stays green while a program
-- of exactly this shape silently loses its hard error.
--
-- THE SHAPE: one file carrying BOTH coherence classes, with the SOFT pair reached
-- FIRST by the scan.
--   * `Tag Bool` twice -- MUTUALLY ⊑ (α-equal heads). A §6 C1 violation: two
--     ⊑-minimal elements, so no minimum at any goal matching them. HARD, and it
--     has no second line of defence -- `entryCovers` is
--     `tyHeadEqV || tyStrictlyMoreSpecificV`, so equal heads cover each other and
--     the goal-site min⊑ reject structurally cannot see this class.
--   * `Tag (Pair Int a)` / `Tag (Pair a Int)` -- ⊑-INCOMPARABLE. A §6.1 condition
--     (a) violation ALONE, which §6.1.2 licenses as at most a declaration-time
--     warning. SOFT.
--
-- WHY THE DECLARATION ORDER IS LOAD-BEARING (do not reorder these four blocks).
-- `checkCoherence` scans `reverseL (… cohRowsOwnedBy …)`, i.e. LATEST-DECLARED
-- FIRST, and takes each element against the rest. With the duplicates declared
-- FIRST and the incomparable pair LAST, the outer element is `Tag (Pair a Int)`
-- and its very first comparison is the SOFT one; the HARD pair is not reached
-- until two outer steps later.
--
-- SPEC ANSWER (hand-derived from §6 C1 and §6.1.2, not captured): the program is
-- REJECTED, because of the duplicate `Tag Bool` heads -- C1 is not relaxed by
-- F-3d and never was (α-equal heads SATISFY condition (a), so they were never
-- (a)'s to demote; §6.1.2's ⚠️ records the ladder breaking exactly there,
-- (a) ⇏ (c)). The incomparable pair is reported too, as a warning, because
-- acceptance under (c) is per-goal and no goal here matches both.
--
-- OBSERVED: check/run/build all REJECT, carrying BOTH `T-CONFLICTING-IMPL` and
-- `W-INCOMPARABLE-IMPLS`. On the PRE-F-3d compiler this file reported ONE
-- diagnostic -- the incomparable pair, as an error -- and NEVER the duplicate,
-- because `cohFirstConflict` returned the first conflict and stopped. So this
-- fixture also records a real (if academic) improvement: the duplicate that the
-- old first-conflict scan hid is now named.
--
-- ⚠️ WHAT MAKES IT A DISCRIMINATOR RATHER THAN A DEMONSTRATION: a one-slot
-- classified `cohScan` -- soft found first, scan stops, nothing else reported --
-- would ACCEPT this file at exit 0 with only a warning, its `main` answering
-- `tag True` by declaration order. The row therefore asserts the ERROR is present,
-- not merely that some diagnostic is.
--
-- ⚠️ `!T-AMBIGUOUS-INSTANCE` is asserted too, and it is not decoration: `main`'s
-- goal `Tag Bool` matches BOTH duplicate impls, so a reader might expect the
-- goal-site reject to fire. It must not -- `entryCovers`' `tyHeadEqV` makes the
-- two cover each other and `findMostSpecificEntry` returns `Some`. That negative
-- is the in-tree pin for "the goal-site arm cannot substitute for the hard
-- declaration-time class", which is the whole reason that class stays an error.
data Pair a b = MkPair a b

interface Tag a where
  tag : a -> Int

impl Tag Bool where
  tag _ = 1

impl Tag Bool where
  tag _ = 2

impl Tag (Pair Int a) where
  tag _ = 3

impl Tag (Pair a Int) where
  tag _ = 4

main = println (tag True)
